result.summary:
Done and left uncommitted. The segment writer's helper thread now syncs through an open file description of its own. Before, it used a try_clone duplicate of the writer's descriptor, so both shared one description. A writeback error is reported once per description: the helper's failed fdatasync took the error, and a run commit whose own fdatasync returned before the helper stored its error was acknowledged over the hole.

The fix: AlignedWriter::helper_description calls reopen(), which opens /proc/self/fd/N (O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC, path built on the stack) once, where the helper is spawned. If that open fails, the writer has no helper and syncs and hashes alone.

The test seam is HelperFault (SegmentOptions::helper_fault, doc(hidden), free when unset). It fails the helper's sync by description, can hold the helper between its fdatasync and the store of its error, uses kcmp to answer the writer's sync with the error when the writer's description is a different one, and can refuse the reopen. Db's run open now ORs its fault byte into the writer's; it used to assign it, which dropped the seam's bit.

Tests added in tests/durable.rs:
- the trace "1 B B Y" at a run commit, plain and under a cipher;
- the same race at a marked segment's sync, at the writer level;
- the fallback when /proc cannot be opened;
- a seal's output whose helper sync failed: no live segment, no manifest names or covers it, flush and settle answer EIO, the seal queue stops, the reopen replays the log;
- a merge's piece whose helper sync failed: nothing lands, the next commit answers EIO, the partition and its pieces stand, the reopen reads the model.

Pricing was done by the run before the restart: no cost resolved. Mutants M3-M9 were run after the restart; all 9 mutants are killed. Docs are done: the CLAUDE.md entry, the ticked ROADMAP item, the stale "dup" comments in src/db.rs and docs/engine.md. WriterHelper::spawn now takes Option<HelperSeams>, because clippy -D warnings refused an 8-argument function. fmt, clippy and the whole checked suite (--no-fail-fast) pass on the landing tree.

The segment writer's helper thread now syncs through an open file description of its own instead of try_clone() of the writer's. The reopen goes through /proc/self/fd/N, O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC, with the path built on the stack, once where the helper is spawned. The kernel therefore reports a writeback error to the writer's description too, and a run commit whose fdatasync returns before the helper stores its error answers the failure (trace "1 B B Y"). If the reopen fails, the writer has no helper and syncs and hashes alone.

The seam is HelperFault (SegmentOptions::helper_fault): it fails the helper's sync by description, can hold the helper between its syscall and its store, and answers the writer's sync where kcmp says its description is another. Db's run open now ORs its fault byte into the writer's instead of assigning it. Tests also cover a seal's output and a merge's piece whose helper sync failed: no manifest names the segment and the landing answers the failure, killed by the join_helper mutant.

Repair round 2: of the reviewers' eight extra mutants (seven distinct), four survived the first pass (R1, R2, R4, R6). Each now has a test:
- a writer with no seam checks via kcmp and fdinfo that the helper's fd is another description, O_RDONLY, CLOEXEC, closed at the finish (kills the production-only try_clone mutant and O_RDWR);
- the width test's access-mode assertion is now == O_RDONLY;
- the writer-level race test runs with the seam armed before and after the writer is made (kills the armed-at-create mutant);
- a lib test checks that an unarmed seam's successful helper syncs count as no failure (kills counted-outside-the-Err).
All seven are killed on the final tree. Round 2 changed tests only. The gate is green: fmt --check, the whole suite with --no-fail-fast (20 binaries), and clippy -D warnings.

The segment writer's helper thread synced through a duplicate of the writer's descriptor (try_clone), which is one open file description. Linux reports a writeback error once per description, so a failed helper fdatasync used up the error for both. A run commit whose own fdatasync returned in the instant before the helper stored its error read the helper's word as clean and was acknowledged over the hole.

Fix (shape 1): AlignedWriter::helper_description -> reopen() opens "/proc/self/fd/<n>" with O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC, the path built on the stack. This is one open where the helper is spawned, and the helper syncs and reads through it, so the kernel also reports the error to the writer's description. If that open fails, the writer runs with no helper (helper_done) and syncs and hashes alone. Db's run open now ORs its fault byte into the writer's instead of assigning it.

New seam HelperFault (SegmentOptions::helper_fault, #[doc(hidden)] pub, free when unset):
- fails the helper's sync by description and records which description it was (kcmp);
- holds the helper between its syscall and its store;
- answers the writer's sync with the error where its description is another;
- can refuse the reopen;
- can start the helper only after the stop.

Tests (tests/durable.rs, section helper-race):
- the trace "1 B B Y" at a run commit, plain and under a cipher, and at the writer;
- the no-/proc fallback, marked and unmarked, at two strides;
- fd widths of 2, 3 and 4 digits;
- the helper's descriptor with no seam (its own description, flags pinned, closed at the finish);
- a sync taken at the stop;
- a seal's output and a merge's piece whose helper sync failed: named by no manifest, the failure is the landing's, the reopen reads the model;
- a test seam on the same writer as a helper seam;
- a failed helper sync is not retried.

Round 3 changed tests and CLAUDE.md only. Of the reviewers' seven mutants, ten variants were run. N2, N3, N4a/b and N6 survived or only hung the first pass; each is now killed by a new or restated test. N7a and N7b survive and are equivalent, argued from the code. Whole suite (--no-fail-fast), fmt --check and clippy -D warnings exit 0 on the final tree.

Pricing ran in rounds 0 and 1 and was not run again this round. It resolved no cost: every cell is level or unresolved, and the stamped phases split between sittings.
